Q2. 
It is possible, in theory, to avoid the trapframe page by storing registers directly on the kernel stack or in the proc structure. 
In the current xv6 design, uservec saves all 32 user registers into a TRAPFRAME page in user space whenever a trap occurs. To eliminate
this per-process TRAPFRAME, uservec could instead push the registers directly onto the kernel stack or copy them into a struct proc field,
such as proc->saved_regs, including registers like ra, sp, s0-s11, a0-a7, t0-t6, and pc. After switching to the kernel page table via satp, 
usertrap can access the saved registers from the kernel memory instead of user space and handle system calls, device interrupts, or exceptions
by calling functions like syscall(), trap(), or kerneltrap(). On returning to user space, usertrapret restores the saved registers from the
kernel stack or proc->saved_regs, resets sepc to the instruction that caused the trap, and switches back to the user page table before executing sret.
Optional adjustments include initializing proc->saved_regs in allocproc() or fork(), ensuring the kernel stack has sufficient space for all registers, 
and maintaining multi-CPU safety if the proc field is accessed on different cores.
Saving registers on the kernel stack would eliminate the need for a mapped trapframe, but the kernel stack is limited in size, and careful management
would be needed to save all registers and temporary values safely. Additionally, the trap handler must have a way to persist these values across traps,
especially if a context switch occurs, which complicates the design. Similarly, writing registers directly into the proc structure would require
the kernel to access memory that is not yet mapped in the current user page table. Since uservec runs before switching to the kernel page table,
attempting to write to proc memory at this stage would likely trigger a page fault, negating the benefit of removing the trapframe page.

Q3. Yes, in principle, xv6 could be modified to eliminate the special TRAMPOLINE page mapping. 
One possible approach would be to copy the trap-handling entry code directly to the kernel stack of each process or to a reserved 
kernel-space buffer accessible by all processes. This way, when a trap occurs, the CPU jumps to an address already valid in the
kernel page table, avoiding reliance on a trampoline mapped into every user address space. The uservec assembly routine would need adjustments
to save registers, switch page tables, and call usertrap entirely from this kernel-accessible memory.
To start, the trap-handling entry point currently in uservec would need to be relocated to a kernel-resident memory area, such as a reserved 
buffer or the kernel stack for each process, instead of being mapped in user space. When a trap occurs, the CPU would jump to this
kernel-accessible trap entry, bypassing the need for the trampoline mapping. The uservec routine would still save all 32 user registers,
but instead of writing them to the TRAPFRAME page, it could push them directly onto the kernel stack or store them in the proc structure 
(struct proc) associated with the current process.
Next, after saving the registers, uservec would switch the page table using satp to the kernel page table and then call usertrap
(defined in kernel/trap.c) to determine the cause of the trap. usertrap would handle system calls, device interrupts, or exceptions
just like in the current design. Any arguments for system calls would still be retrieved from the saved registers using existing helper functions
such as argint() or argstr(). Returning from the trap would involve a modified usertrapret routine, which restores the saved registers from the
kernel stack or proc structure and executes sret to return to user space.
It must ensure that the trap entry code is always valid in the current page table at the moment of the trap.  
Overall, while removing the TRAMPOLINE mapping is feasible, but the trampoline is a simple, reliable way to handle traps safely.

